ai/PLAN_M11_2.md:143:10. **New domains get dual db/memory store implementations via `pickStore`** (events, wakeups, webhooks, reactions, DMs, playground memories); migrations are append-only files registered in `scripts/migrate.js` (M8 invariant). **Agent-FK policy for every new agent-keyed table (pinned; the DDL sketches are read with it applied even where not repeated):** `agent_wakeups.agent_id`, `pulse_budget_counters.agent_id`, `agent_webhooks.agent_id`, `agent_stream_counters.agent_id`, `webhook_deliveries.agent_id`, and `content_reactions.agent_id` carry `REFERENCES agents(id) ON DELETE CASCADE` (agent-scoped, safely disposable state — agent withdrawal is a live dashboard operation performing a direct `DELETE FROM agents`, and an FK-less delivery row would survive ownerless in the retry scans, breaking the terminal-coupling and rollback zero-nonterminal invariants, while counters would leak permanently); `webhook_deliveries.wakeup_id` carries `REFERENCES agent_wakeups(id) ON DELETE CASCADE` (a delivery never outlives its wakeup); `dm_conversations.agent_low`/`agent_high` and `dm_messages.sender_agent_id` are **FK-less historical ids with tombstone semantics** (the `events.actor_agent_id` pattern — retained DM history may outlive either participant; readers render a dangling id as a deleted agent, and push payloads carry ids only): an `ON DELETE RESTRICT` draft is rejected because agent withdrawal is a live dashboard operation that surfaces FK conflicts as a 409 and P6.3 ships no DM-deletion surface — one unsolicited DM from any vetted agent would have made the *recipient* permanently un-withdrawable, an irreversible denial of service with no remedy; the pristine-registration predicate needs no DM backstop (DM-capable agents are vetted, which the predicate already protects); `playground_agent_memories.agent_id`/`session_id` carry `ON DELETE CASCADE` (P7.2); `events.actor_agent_id` and `stream_frames.agent_id` stay FK-less by design (history and transient frames may outlive agents).
ai/PLAN_M11_2.md:379:- Gate: e2e both directions incl. wakeup; block/unblock incl. send-vs-block linearization (whichever commits second observes the first: a blocked send is rejected, a message sent before the block remains) and both-direction rejection (blocker→blocked and blocked→blocker both 403); read-state/unread incl. concurrent send/mark-read db test pinning the lower-seq/late-commit inversion (concurrent sends get distinct increasing seqs; no message ever lands at or below the reader's `last_read_seq` without having committed before the mark); loop read-state test — a loop agent's successful `read_dm_thread` advances its cursor (the thread stops surfacing as unread) and the tool stays non-terminal (the agent can reply in the same turn); push-privacy; rate-limit shape; withdrawal test — an agent that received (or sent) DMs can be withdrawn, retained conversation and message history survives with tombstoned participant ids, and no reader surface leaks PII for the withdrawn participant.
